//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// `print` reads its operand where the program is running, so a value the active
// topology cannot see is refused -- the same question `o[0][0]` is refused on.
//
// The question used to be asked only of an identifier, so the asymmetry was
// narrower than it looked: `print(o)` on a binding was already refused, while
// `print(mk())` on a call returning the same placed type was accepted. That one
// lowers to MLIR's `printMemrefF32`, which walks the memref on the host; with a
// device present the data pointer is device memory and the walk faults, which
// is what a rented A100 found the first time anything ran that far.
//
// Refusing is the whole fix. The transfer home already exists and works, and
// every program that hits this has a one-line correction that makes its data
// movement a priced edge instead of a side effect of printing.
//
// `GPU_HBM` is `managed: explicit` here on purpose. A space left to the default
// is `cached`, and a cached space is readable across the boundary by the same
// M5 rule that governs every other implicit cross-space use -- so refusing it
// would refuse what the model permits everywhere else.
//
// RUN: not vxc %s 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: E6003
// CHECK-SAME: `print` reads its argument on CPU
// CHECK-SAME: the value lives in GPU_HBM
// CHECK-SAME: transfer(.., Memory::CPU_DRAM)

Memory CPU_DRAM {}
Memory GPU_HBM {
  capacity: 40 GiB, bandwidth: 1555 GB/s, managed: explicit
}
Topology Dev {
  arch: nvptx64,
  memory: Memory::GPU_HBM,
  visible: [ Memory::GPU_HBM ],
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::GPU_HBM : 10,
  transfer Memory::GPU_HBM -> Memory::CPU_DRAM : 10
}

fn mk() -> Tensor<f32, [4, 4], Memory::GPU_HBM> {
  let h = Tensor<f32, [4, 4]>::uninit();
  return transfer(h, Memory::GPU_HBM);
}

fn main() -> i32 {
  print(mk());
  return 0;
}
